---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import DotMatrix from '../components/visual/DotMatrix.astro'
import CameraCrop from '../components/visual/CameraCrop.astro'
import MiniBoard from '../components/visual/MiniBoard.astro'
import { CHANNEL, EVIDENCE } from '../data/channel'
import { abs, SOCIAL } from '../lib/site'

const breadcrumb = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'About', item: abs('/about/') }],
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Total Football explains the game as a diagram: every frame drawn, nothing filmed. Here is the method, and the measurements behind it."
  path="/about/"
  structuredData={[breadcrumb]}
>
  <section class="stage border-b border-ink-hair">
    <div class="shell grid gap-14 py-20 md:py-28 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr] lg:items-center">
      <div class="max-w-[42rem]">
        <p class="micro mb-7" data-reveal="fade">About</p>
        <h1 class="text-display font-black" data-reveal="clip" data-reveal-delay="80">
          A whiteboard that moves.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-9 max-w-prose text-xl leading-[1.6] text-ink/85" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="220">
          Total Football started from a simple frustration: the best tactical ideas in the game are
          explained with a still image and an arrow, or with a clip that cuts away before the
          interesting part happens.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal="rise" data-reveal-delay="160">
        <MiniBoard class="mx-auto block w-full max-w-[300px]" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="grid gap-14 md:grid-cols-[0.85fr_1.15fr]">
      <div>
        <p class="micro mb-6" data-reveal="fade">The method</p>
        <h2 class="text-title font-black" data-reveal="clip">
          Drawn, so the whole shape is visible.
        </h2>
        <figure class="mt-10" data-reveal="rise" data-reveal-delay="140">
          <CameraCrop class="w-full" />
          <figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            What a camera gives you, against what is actually on the pitch.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="max-w-prose space-y-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85">
        <p data-reveal>
          Every breakdown is animated from scratch on a scale pitch. Players are counters, the ball
          is locked to whoever has it, and a camera follows the action the way your eye would. Because
          it is drawn rather than filmed, the ten players away from the ball are always on screen,
          and they are usually the ones doing the interesting work.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also means a moment can be held while a principle is stated, rewound, and run a second
          time with exactly one variable changed. That two-takes device is impossible with footage
          and it is the clearest way to show that a decision, not an accident, produced the outcome.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing is borrowed from a broadcaster. That is partly a matter of principle and partly a
          practical one: the football channels that live on clipped footage spend their lives being
          taken down.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-ink-hair bg-paper-deep/40">
    <div class="shell py-20">
      <p class="micro mb-12" data-reveal="fade">What the numbers say</p>
      <dl class="grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-3">
        {
          EVIDENCE.map((e, i) => (
            <div data-reveal="rise" data-reveal-delay={i * 90}>
              <dt class="mb-4 text-4xl font-black tabular-nums md:text-5xl">{e.stat}</dt>
              <dd>
                <p class="text-[17px] font-semibold leading-snug">{e.label}</p>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{e.note}</p>
              </dd>
              {/* The first stat is a proportion, so it gets drawn as one:
                  23 shorts, 7 of them past 20k plays. */}
              {i === 0 && (
                <DotMatrix
                  total={23}
                  filled={7}
                  class="mt-6 w-full max-w-[220px]"
                  label="Seven of twenty-three system clinics passed twenty thousand plays"
                />
              )}
              {i === 1 && (
                <div class="mt-6 max-w-[220px]" aria-hidden="true">
                  <div class="h-2 w-full overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-ink-hair">
                    <div class="h-full w-[65%] rounded-full bg-tf-gradient" />
                  </div>
                  <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-ink-faint">
                    of the runtime, watched
                  </p>
                </div>
              )}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-12 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Every editorial rule on this channel came out of a measurement rather than a hunch. When the
        data said the hot-take format was not working, it was abandoned, even though it was easier
        to make.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="grid gap-14 md:grid-cols-[0.85fr_1.15fr]">
      <div>
        <p class="micro mb-6">Where to find it</p>
        <h2 class="text-title font-black">{CHANNEL.followersLabel} and growing.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="max-w-prose space-y-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85">
        <p>
          New breakdowns go out on social first and land in the library here afterwards, laid out so
          you can read them at your own pace instead of at twenty seconds a system.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/library/" class="font-semibold underline underline-offset-4">Browse the library</a>
          {
            SOCIAL.facebook && (
              <>
                {' '}or{' '}
                <a
                  href={SOCIAL.facebook}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="font-semibold underline underline-offset-4"
                >
                  follow on Facebook
                </a>
              </>
            )
          }.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
